index f5686cc84..508ce0767 100644
--- a/docs/architecture/versioning.md
+++ b/docs/architecture/versioning.md
@@ -13,7 +13,7 @@ How `callisto version` turns changesets into file edits, and where interrupted-c
 - A changelog section is `## {version}`, a date and compare-link line, then Major, Minor and Patch Changes groups (`[changelog] layout = "grouped"`, the default), or the heading and one bullet list (`"flat"`). `render_section` is pure: the planner puts the layout, the UTC date and the link in `ChangelogInput::style`, and the link needs a GitHub repository (`[release] forge-repository`, else the `origin` remote) plus a tag on the previous version. Readers (`find_section`, `has_heading`, release notes) key on the `## {version}` line only, so they read both layouts; a fixed group's release notes file each member's entries under the bump group the member recorded them in.
 - `plan_version` is shared: `version`, `status`, `compose-pr-body` and `release plan --package` all compute the same plan.
 - `crates/callisto-graph/src/changelog/markdown/` is the only place Markdown is scanned: fenced code, ATX headings, heading rebase, the headline split and `Breaking:`/`Action:` markers (`markers.rs`). A new pass asks those questions there and joins `corpus.rs`; it does not write its own line scanner.
-- `ReleasePreview` (`crates/callisto-graph/src/release_preview.rs`) is the serializable, versioned view of a plan for people: packages, merged changes, bystanders and findings. Surfaces such as the PR body render it; they do not recompute from the plan. `version --emit-preview` writes it before apply consumes the changesets, and `compose-pr-body --preview` renders from that file. `ReleasePreview` also carries findings from the changesets (a `Breaking:` or `Action:` marker, a breaking marker whose bump is too low, and no \"backward compatible\" claim when there is a breaking one) and `unreleased`, the publishable packages with source changes since their last tag that the plan does not bump (`version --emit-preview` computes it; a failure to look is a warning). `pr_labels()` derives the managed label catalog, with an `applied` flag per label, from the same findings. `--publish-plan` adds `publish`, the outcome of `plan_workspace_release_offline` on the versioned tree for exactly the packages the preview bumps (the committed decision holds those, not every unreleased package). It never observes a registry or the forge, so the checks that need one (a published version left behind, a name another project owns) are left to the release itself; a refusal becomes a `Stop` finding. Rows are keyed by the qualified release identity, so `cargo/x` and `npm/x` stay apart.
+- `ReleasePreview` (`crates/callisto-graph/src/release_preview.rs`) is the serializable, versioned view of a plan for people: packages, merged changes, bystanders and findings. Surfaces such as the PR body render it; they do not recompute from the plan. `version --emit-preview` writes it before apply consumes the changesets, and `compose-pr-body --preview` renders from that file. Each changeset change records `pr`, the pull request that added it, read from the first-parent merge commits in one `git log` when the history names one. `ReleasePreview` also carries findings from the changesets (a `Breaking:` or `Action:` marker, a breaking marker whose bump is too low, and no \"backward compatible\" claim when there is a breaking one) and `unreleased`, the publishable packages with source changes since their last tag that the plan does not bump (`version --emit-preview` computes it; a failure to look is a warning). `pr_labels()` derives the managed label catalog, with an `applied` flag per label, from the same findings. `--publish-plan` adds `publish`, the outcome of `plan_workspace_release_offline` on the versioned tree for exactly the packages the preview bumps (the committed decision holds those, not every unreleased package). It never observes a registry or the forge, so the checks that need one (a published version left behind, a name another project owns) are left to the release itself; a refusal becomes a `Stop` finding. Rows are keyed by the qualified release identity, so `cargo/x` and `npm/x` stay apart.
 - `changed_source_files_since_last_tag` lists, per package, the files that differ from its last tag after dropping `DEFAULT_IGNORED_PATHS` (tests, benches, examples, snapshots, Markdown), and `uncovered_changes` returns the changed packages that no pending changeset, nor any member of their fixed or linked group, covers. Neither is wired into the CLI or the PR body yet.
 - A dry run never gets a permit, so it reports the plan and never calls apply.
 - `snapshot` applies with `ApplyOptions::transient`: manifests are written, while changelogs, changeset deletion and git staging are skipped.
